# run.log — 2026-10-15-area-l4-ledger-reader — ZERO requests, offline pass
#
# There is no per-request table here because no request was sent. The columns the
# repo's ledger format requires (seq / method / status / t_queued / t_sent / t_done)
# have zero rows to carry. What this file records instead is each tool invocation
# this pass made, its exit status, and what it read.
#
# 0 of a 0 cap. Methods: none (GET 0, OPTIONS 0, POST 0). Delay: >=2s rule, not
# applicable, 0 requests sent, so no inter-request gap exists to report.
#
# LINE COUNTS. Three rules, all stated because getting any of them wrong makes a
# counted file uncounted:
#   1. grep -c '' is used, not wc -l. wc -l on a capture with no trailing newline
#      reports one line fewer than was printed — that understated every count in
#      an earlier revision of this file.
#   2. Captures are 2>&1, so a count is stdout + stderr TOGETHER. Only self-test
#      writes to stderr in normal operation (one UNREADABLE line, by design: the
#      integration negative control proves the reader refuses, and it says so on
#      stderr). So self-test's line count is its stdout + 1.
#   3. The invocation column names each command exactly as `probe.sh` runs it,
#      `python3 ` prefix included. An earlier revision dropped the prefix for
#      `read-area-l.py`, which is not executable — the column then named commands
#      that would have exited 127.
#
## Correction (2026-10-17) — one recorded count moved, and only because a heading was added

Correction issue #139's eight findings were re-derived at head by a later, separate
pass. One recorded count moved: `sh dup-test.sh` goes **71 -> 72**, because the
exempt-section listing prints one line per Correction heading and the log now carries
one more of them. Same cause as section 8a-7's 70 -> 71, and no figure moved. Every
other line count in the table below is unchanged and was RE-measured, not carried:
each invocation was re-run with `2>&1 | grep -c ''` immediately before this commit,
because a transcript is printed, never transcribed (verifier round 4's lesson). The
Correction heading is what the repo's own append-only check
(scripts/research-checks.py) requires before it will accept an edit to any file
under findings-log/, and it is why dup-test.sh's CORR_RE now also recognises
`## Correction (YYYY-MM-DD)` — see that file's own Correction note.

## Correction (2026-10-17) — second run of this date: `sh dup-test.sh` 72 → 73

A fresh-context verifier found two prose defects in PR #147 (both fixed, both
recorded in `FINDINGS.md`'s second `## Correction (2026-10-17)` section). This
table moves once more, for the same single cause as 70 → 71 → 72: the
exempt-section listing prints one line per Correction heading, and the log now
carries one more of them. **73 lines, measured, not carried** — re-run with
`2>&1 | grep -c ''` immediately before the commit that adds this revision. Every
other count above is unchanged at 27 / 37 / 9 / 264 / 52 / 33 / 53, each
re-measured in the same pass. No figure moved; nothing was re-probed; 0 requests.

The two corrections are worth one line here too, because both are the same failure
this pass keeps paying for: **a statement that does not survive being run.** One
said *four* rounds beside seven commits; one published a `grep` recipe with no `-r`
that returned `Is a directory`, exit 2, and no rows. A third and fourth, found while
fixing the first two, were an escaped alternation that matched nothing and a recipe
with no file argument that read stdin. **A recipe that returns nothing is not
evidence of a negative** — the discriminator is the exit code, measured on this host
with grep (GNU grep 3.12): **1** for a pattern that ran and matched nothing, **2** for
a usage error (the no-`-r` directory form is one — `Is a directory` exits **2**),
**0** for a match; the separation is 1 against 2. ⚠ This copy first read "0 for
"ran and matched nothing", 2 for a usage error" — the same inversion round 2 fixed
in FINDINGS.md's second Correction section, and which this file's copy of the
sentence survived; both copies state the measured values now.

